lesson chemistry-weathering-and-erosion v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=9 subject=chemistry levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Weathering and erosion"
  goal "Say how ice, roots and rain break rock, and how the river and the waves carry the pieces and wear them round."

  look {
    scene 34x14 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 0) space=2 align=end {
        rocks r rocks=["sandstone", "granite"] test=1 letters=0
        soiljar j stones=1 sand=3 silt=2 clay=1 bits=3 names=0
      }
    }
    say """
      Ben walks the fossil cliffs after a hard winter. A slab has come down onto the beach, split
      along a crack that was full of rain when the frost came. Ice takes up more room than water
      and pushes the rock apart; roots push too, as they grow; and the rain, a very weak acid,
      eats chalk away a grain at a time. That is weathering: rock breaking where it stands. Then
      the river and the waves carry the pieces off and knock them smaller and rounder, until a
      cliff is a beach of pebbles and, further on, sand. That is erosion.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked chem.frost-cracks k=0 p=0
    }
    practice chem.frost-cracks count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice chem.match-cause count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice chem.cliff-to-sand count=1 seed=3 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice chem.oldest-layer count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice chem.river-table count=2 easy-count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice chem.rock-soaks count=1 hard-count=0 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    level hard {
      show chem.match-cause k=0 r=2 level=medium
    }
  }
  story {
    show chem.years-of-rain r=4 y=25 w=0 easy-r=2 easy-y=10 hard-r=5 hard-d=2 hard-w=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show chem.beach-pebbles k=0 r=1 hard-k=1 hard-r=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show chem.cliff-in-a-hundred-years k=0 r=2 hard-k=1 hard-r=1
  }
  remember {
    say "Ice, roots and rain break rock where it stands. Rivers and waves carry the pieces and wear them round."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea comes in two halves that the words keep apart: weathering breaks rock where it is
    (ice swelling in a crack, roots growing in it, rain dissolving chalk), and erosion carries
    the pieces away and wears them (the river, the waves, the wind). The look is the drop test on
    two rocks beside the settled jar, and the way in asks only which rock the frost can crack,
    which is the one that soaked the drop up. The core matches causes to what they do, orders the
    steps from crack to sand, reads the jar for the oldest layer, and reads a table of pieces
    along the river. The wrong idea to watch for is that the sea dissolves pebbles round, or that
    round stones were always round; ask what a wave does to a pebble a thousand times a day. The
    story counts the centimetres the waves take, or the years until the edge reaches the path.
    The two-star question asks why beach pebbles are round when hill stones are sharp, and the
    three-star question predicts a cliff in a hundred years from two clues about its rock and its
    weather. If the child cannot say which rock the frost cracks, stay here another day with a
    sponge, a stone and the freezer.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the frost's rock is worked once in the teacher's pen before a row of three with one that soaks, the causes are ice, roots and rain, the steps start nearly in order, the layers asked are the oldest and the youngest, the river's table is read for the smallest pieces, the story multiplies two centimetres by ten years, the pebbles are the beach against the hill, and the cliff is the soaking one by the sea."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the rows hold two rocks that keep water out beside the one that soaks, the causes are the river, the waves and the wind, a second match with the weathering causes sits in the core, the steps come reversed, the layers are asked for the youngest and through a fossil in the middle, which is younger than the bottom's, the river's table is read for the sharp pieces and the ones carried furthest, the story asks the years until a path two metres back is reached at five centimetres a year, the pebbles are the sand at the river's mouth, and the cliff is the granite in the dry valley."
  }
}
